===South Korea vs Germany===
[[File:South Korea vs Germany 2018 World Cup 15.jpg|thumb|The German team.]]
The two teams had faced each other three times, including two [[FIFA World Cup]] games, one at the [[1994 FIFA World Cup]] group stage, a Germany [[1994 FIFA World Cup Group C#Germany vs South Korea|3–2]] victory.<ref name=Kit12/> and one at the [[2002 FIFA World Cup]] semi-finals, which ended with a [[2002 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Germany vs South Korea|1–0]] victory for Germany, and despite South Korea's two previous losses, they were not eliminated just yet. [[File:South Korea vs Germany 2018 World Cup 13.jpg|thumb|left|The South Korean team.]]For South Korea to have any chance of advancing, Mexico would have to defeat Sweden in their match that same day, and South Korea would then have to defeat Germany by at least two goals, a feat predicted to be highly improbable going in, with the South Korean coach before the game predicting that it had a "one percent" chance of happening.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.firstpost.com/sports/fifa-world-cup-2018-south-korea-have-one-percent-chance-of-staying-in-the-tournament-says-midfielder-ju-se-jong-4600471.html|title=FIFA World Cup 2018: South Korea have one percent chance of staying in the tournament, says midfielder Ju Se-jong|website=Firstpost}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L3NxoRSztmE|title=Guus Hiddink's Reaction on South Korea Defeating Germany &#124; 거스 히딩크 감독의 카잔의 기적 반응|date=27 June 2018|via=YouTube}}</ref>

[[Son Heung-min]] shot wide from the edge of the penalty area before [[Marco Reus]] shot one towards goal at the other end only to see it blocked. [[Leon Goretzka]] drew a one-handed save from [[Jo Hyeon-woo]] with a header from the middle of the penalty area, the South Korean goalkeeper diving to his right and pushing the ball away at full stretch. Two minutes into the second-half's stoppage time, Son's corner from the left reached [[Kim Young-gwon]] at the far post who scored into the right corner from six yards out with his left foot, the offside decision that followed was overruled by a VAR decision.<ref>{{cite web |last=Creek |first=Stephen |title=Defending champions crash out after Kazan collapse |url=http://www.goal.com/en-sg/news/3868/main/2018/06/28/45902092/south-korea-2-germany-0-defending-champions-crash-out-after |website=Goal.com |date=27 June 2018}}</ref> [[Ju Se-jong]] won the ball off [[Manuel Neuer]] who had been at the other end of the pitch in order to join the German attack, thus leaving the goal undefended, and hit a long ball to Son, who ran onto the ball and tapped into an empty net from close range to score the second.<ref name="KOR-GER recap">{{cite web |url=https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/review-korea-republic--germany-2971533 |title=Germany crash out as Korea Republic end on high |publisher=FIFA |date=27 June 2018 |access-date=27 June 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520052245/https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/review-korea-republic--germany-2971533 |archive-date=20 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="BBC South Korea v Germany">{{cite web |last=Emons |first=Michael |title=South Korea 2 Germany 0 |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44439270 |work=BBC Sport |date=27 June 2018}}</ref> Had Mexico defeated Sweden in their final group stage match that same day, a South Korean victory over Germany by at least two goals would have advanced South Korea to the round of 16 based on goal differential, at the expense of Sweden. However, since Sweden defeated Mexico 3–0, the South Koreans were eliminated despite their 2–0 victory over Germany. This marked the second time that South Korea failed to qualify for the knockout stage in consecutive World Cups, having been eliminated in the group stage at the previous [[2014 FIFA World Cup|tournament]] and in four consecutive World Cups from 1986 to 1998.

[[File:South Korea vs Germany 2018 World Cup 40.jpg|thumb|Goal for South Korea.]]
Germany were the fourth defending champions to be eliminated from the group stage at the World Cup in the last five tournaments, following [[France national football team|France]] in [[2002 FIFA World Cup|2002]], [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]], and [[Spain national football team|Spain]] in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]]; and since the new millennium, all champions eliminated in the group stage had been [[UEFA]] members. This was only the second time that Germany had been eliminated from the first round at the World Cup, having done so in [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]], while also being the first time since then that Germany failed to finish among the best eight teams of the tournament. However, this was the first time it happened after the first round was converted back to a group stage format in [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]]. This was Germany's first ever defeat against an Asian nation in a World Cup match in what was their sixth such match, and also the first time that Germany suffered two defeats in a same World Cup since [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]] (in which Germany lost by [[2010 FIFA World Cup Group D#Germany vs Serbia|1–0]] for [[Serbia national football team|Serbia]] in group stage and also by [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Germany vs Spain|1–0]] for [[Spain national football team|Spain]] in the semi-finals). This was South Korea's first victory (and [[clean sheet]]) at the World Cup since their [[2010 FIFA World Cup Group B#South Korea vs Greece|2–0]] win over Greece in 2010. Son's goal for South Korea (95:52) was the latest goal Germany had ever conceded in a 90-minute World Cup match.<ref name="BBC South Korea v Germany"/> This was also the first time Germany finished bottom in the World Cup's group stage, one of the worst performances in their history; having been eliminated from the group stage of [[UEFA Euro 2000]] and [[UEFA Euro 2004|2004]]. South Korea's win also meant that [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]] was the only team from the [[Asian Football Confederation]] that failed to win a game in their 2018 World Cup campaign. Germany would also suffer the same fate again 4 years later in [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]], this time finishing 3rd of their group stage.

